\section{Alias source slugs}{

\code{code_system} names the identifier's code system; the slug passed to
\code{\link[=resolve_polity_label]{resolve_polity_label()}} is built per row from it:
\itemize{
\item \code{"usda-nass-fips"}: the state FIPS code
\code{\link[=read_admin_stats_nass]{read_admin_stats_nass()}} emits. Slug as given.
\item \code{"eurostat-nuts"}: the NUTS code \code{\link[=read_admin_stats_eurostat]{read_admin_stats_eurostat()}}
emits. Slug \code{"eurostat-nuts<nuts_version>"}, e.g.
\code{"eurostat-nuts2016"}.
\item \code{"jrc-nuts"}: the NUTS code of the JRC subnational release
(\code{inst/extdata/jrc_subnational_source_manifest.csv}), coded on NUTS
2016. Slug \code{"jrc-nuts<nuts_version>"}.
\item \code{"ibge-uf"}: the UF code \code{\link[=read_admin_stats_sidra]{read_admin_stats_sidra()}} emits. Slug as
given.
\item \code{"whep-lab-<family>"}: the compilation's own \code{admin_unit_id}, as
\code{\link[=read_admin_family]{read_admin_family()}} emits it. Slug as given.
}

The five \code{"whep-lab-"} slugs are the five tier-2/3 families
\code{\link[=read_admin_family]{read_admin_family()}} reads, with the \code{"admin-stats-"} prefix replaced:
\code{"whep-lab-japan"}, \code{"whep-lab-spain-provinces"},
\code{"whep-lab-australia"}, \code{"whep-lab-france-livestock"} and
\code{"whep-lab-latam"}. Their identifiers are the compilation's own
(\code{"JPN-AICHI"}, \code{"ESP-ES111"}, \code{"FRA-FR102"}), which is why each family
is its own code system rather than a shared one.

Identifiers are normalised exactly as \code{\link[=resolve_polity_label]{resolve_polity_label()}}
normalises a label, on both sides of the comparison, so the injected
and published routes agree on what a key is.
}

